; The shop's two claims, re-asked as derivations (extension §2). Checked AGAINST
; jobshop.claims by modality-cross-check.sh, never instead of it: two
; implementations of one question, and a disagreement is a bug in one of them.
;
; POLARITY — the same rule river.rules spells out, and the same trap.
;   `possible F` is POSITIVE: the relation holds the reachable situations that
;   satisfy F, so NON-EMPTY means the property holds.
;   `live F` is AG EF F, which no stratified relation can say positively, so it
;   is the COUNTEREXAMPLE SET: the reachable situations from which no F is
;   reachable. EMPTY means the property holds. Writing it as "the schedules that
;   can still finish" would read as the satisfying set and silently invert the
;   oracle — every live verdict backwards, the script still green.

(load "../libraries/scheduling.lib.pol")

; Reachability over real edges, reflexive at every situation.
(load "ct.rules")

; `possible`: the schedules, reachable from the empty shop, in which every job
; is done. Non-empty ⟺ `holds all-finish`.
(satisfies all-finish (all-done j))

; `live`: the counterexample set. `all-finish` is F; `can-still-finish` is EF F;
; `never-stuck` is the reachable schedules where EF F fails — the deadlocks.
; Non-empty ⟺ `fails never-stuck`. The F was written out twice before, once
; here and once as `finished`; it is one guard and now has one name.
(can-reach can-still-finish all-finish)
(trapped never-stuck can-still-finish)

; The per-machine view the conjunctions above cannot give: who holds what, in a
; given situation. `pol derive jobshop.pol jobshop.rules "(holds-machine S M J)"`
; reads a deadlock off the space. The typed declaration is required, not
; decorative: `M` and `J` occur only in sort-transparent positions here, so
; nothing else in the rule could seed them.
(relation holds-machine (Situation machine job))
(rule (holds-machine S M J) (situation S) (holds S (is M.held-by J)))
